<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Giardini Naxos</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Giardini Naxos is a seaside town in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a> on the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, below <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taormina">Taormina</a>. It stands on the site of Naxos, founded in 734 BCE as the first Greek colony in Sicily, and an archaeological park on the Schisò headland preserves its walls, sanctuaries and street grid. Today it is a resort town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giardini-naxos.jpg" alt="Giardini Naxos" width="320" height="296" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeanne Griffin · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giardini_Naxos_panorama_23-01-2023_cropped_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Giardini Naxos, literally Naxos Gardens and called I Jardina in Sicilian, is a comune in the Metropolitan City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a>. It lies on the coast of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-sea">Ionian Sea</a>, on a bay between Cape Taormina and Cape Schisò, with the town of Taormina in the hills above it that can easily be reached by car and bus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Naxos was founded in 734 BCE by Thucles the Chalcidian, in the part of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-graecia">Magna Graecia</a>. It was never a powerful city, but its temple of Apollo Archegetes, the protecting deity of all the Greek colonies, gave it prominence in religious affairs. Leontini and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catania">Catania</a> were both colonised from Naxos. Hippocrates, tyrant of Gela, captured the city in 494 BCE. Its opposition to Syracuse led in the end to its capture and destruction in 403 BCE by the tyrant Dionysius, after it had supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in that city's disastrous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-expedition">Sicilian Expedition</a>. The site continued to be inhabited, but most activity shifted to the neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taormina">Tauromenium</a>.</p><p>In 1544, after raids by the corsair Kheir-ed-Din, several military buildings were constructed to protect Cape Schisò from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> pirates, who continued to attack and plunder the coastal villages. They were Schisò Castle, rebuilt from an earlier castle of the 13th century, Schisò fort and Vignazza Tower. The tower is a quadrangular watchtower that patrolled the coast south of Port Schisò, and observers inside it could alert the villagers and neighbouring watchtowers with smoke signals if pirate boats were sighted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Until the early 1970s Giardini Naxos was a quiet fishing village. It is now a seaside resort and a stop for cruise ships, with beaches, a small fishing port, a larger port for the cruise ships, several churches and an archaeological park. Many Italians own summer homes there, and every Ferragosto people spend the night camping and celebrating on the beaches.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Giardini Naxos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Excavations at <b>Giardini Naxos</b> have revealed the walls, sanctuaries and street grid.</blockquote><a href="/state/naxos-sicily">Naxos (Sicily)</a></li></ul></section>
